# DEFAULT-CA: globalThis.__tjs_set_default_ca(pem) REPLACES the CA set every engine client trusts by default, for the
# connections made from now on (node's tls.setDefaultCACertificates).
#
# WHY. Native Claude Code builds its own CA list (its bundled roots MINUS nineteen it blocks by certificate hash, plus
# NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS and the OS store), hands it to tls.setDefaultCACertificates, and passes the same list to every
# fetch as Bun's `tls: { ca }`. The engine took neither: fetch, XMLHttpRequest and WebSocket (lws) trusted the
# compiled-in roots whatever the caller asked, and four of the roots native refuses (emSign / eMudhra) are among them.
# So quaude trusted servers native Claude Code does not (measured 2026-11-23, 2.1.330, darwin-arm64; the probe and the
# Bun oracle are in test/node-shim-tls-options.test.cjs and test/fixtures/bun-tls-option-reads-native.json).
#
# WHAT. The whole set is parsed first (one certificate that fails, or none: TypeError, nothing changed, since lws
# refuses a set with any bad block). mod_tls: a new chain, used by every tjs.connect('tls') without a `ca` of its own;
# earlier chains are kept until the runtime ends (a configured connection points at its own). lws: the set replaces
# ca_bundle_data and bumps a generation; tjs__lws_select_vhost makes new client vhosts (same proxy settings, kept from
# init) at the next connection, and leaves the old ones to the requests already on them. Callable any number of times.
# libexec/node-shim/modules/tls.cjs answers setDefaultCACertificates with it; scripts/engine-api-floor.cjs holds it.
# private.h, mod_tls.c, lws-utils.c, vm.c; generated against the fully patched tree, so LAST.
#
